 ## 2.4 Related work and the status of the contribution
 
-This article offers a bounded integrative synthesis. HAIG examines the distribution of decision authority, process autonomy, and accountability in human–AI arrangements (Engin, 2026). Reviewable automated decision-making situates records and accountability across the sociotechnical process (Cobbe et al., 2021), while contestable AI by design specifies features and practices supporting intervention and challenge (Alfrink et al., 2023). Sebastian et al. (2026) address organizational decision rights in relation to ambiguity and risk.
-
-The Institutional Decision Episode (IDE) makes selected distinctions explicit in a common record for one decision or action, or for linked episodes. Its potential incremental utility is clearest where an analyst must distinguish a change of institutional competence from a change in operational permissions, or deliberative approval from subsequent execution. The B4 reconstruction illustrates how the scheme can preserve these distinctions. It does not show that other approaches cannot represent them or that the scheme improves analysis in practice. Where an existing framework answers the question at the required resolution, the IDE may add no value.
+This article offers a bounded integrative synthesis. HAIG examines configurations of decision authority, process autonomy, and accountability in human–AI arrangements (Engin, 2026). Reviewable automated decision-making examines decisions through records and points of review and intervention across the sociotechnical process (Cobbe et al., 2021). Contestable AI by design addresses features and practices that make intervention and challenge possible throughout the system lifecycle (Alfrink et al., 2023). Sebastian et al. (2026) address organizational decision rights in relation to ambiguity and risk.
+
+These approaches overlap in substance with the IDE, although their units and aims differ: a governance configuration, a reviewable process, or conditions for contestation in system design, as distinct from a bounded institutional episode. The IDE brings authority, mandate, capability, enablement, action, evidence, contestability, and disposition into one record and links records when the relevant actor, competence, or action changes. The contribution is this explicit integration. Where another approach reaches the same diagnosis at the required resolution, additional detail in the IDE record does not establish additional analytical value (Section 8).
 
